<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Peloponnesian Senate</h1><p class="st-meta">1821 CE – 1822 CE · capital Tripolitsa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Revolutionary governing body of the Peloponnese in the first year of the Greek War of Independence, dominated by the region's notables.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When the Greek revolt broke out in March 1821, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrobey-mavromichalis">Petros Mavromichalis</a> formed the Messenian Senate at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalamata">Kalamata</a>. In May and June 1821 leading notables and clergy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> met at the monastery of Kaltezes and created the Peloponnesian Senate to direct the war and administer liberated territory, later based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli-greece">Tripolitsa</a> after its capture.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The senate was controlled by the Peloponnesian primates (kodjabashis), local landowners and tax-farmers who had held power under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrobey-mavromichalis">Petrobey Mavromichalis</a> as president. It organised supplies and taxes for the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli-greece">Tripolitsa</a> and quarrelled with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrios-ypsilantis">Demetrios Ypsilantis</a>, who claimed national leadership, and with military chiefs such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoros-kolokotronis">Theodoros Kolokotronis</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The First <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a> at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/epidaurus">Epidaurus</a> in January 1822 created a central Provisional Administration, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/first-hellenic-republic">First Hellenic Republic</a>. The regional senate was subordinated and soon dissolved, though the rivalry between notables and military leaders continued into the civil wars of 1823-1825.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/peloponnesian-senate.jpg" alt="Peloponnesian Senate" width="320" height="316"><figcaption>Image: Geraki · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seal_of_the_Peloponnesian_Senate.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1821 CE – 1822 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tripolitsa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Petros Mavromichalis</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Senate of the Peloponnese, Messenian Senate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1822&amp;focus=peloponnesian-senate" aria-label="Peloponnesian Senate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5224 747 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Peloponnesian Senate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0M5570 1082h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5544 1003h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5544 1003h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Greece. <a href="/atlas?year=1822&amp;focus=peloponnesian-senate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
